WEBVTT

00:00:00.218 --> 00:00:01.181
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> Part 2

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:05.018
<v><b>R:</b></v> Erm, and then, of course, as you say, life is full of stories,

00:00:05.043 --> 00:00:07.336
life is full of adventures and misadventures, light and shade,

00:00:07.349 --> 00:00:10.766
and then you had this devastating diagnosis of cancer,

00:00:10.785 --> 00:00:14.150
um, and that, for a moment, put you completely out of the game.

00:00:14.704 --> 00:00:20.798
<v><b>J:</b></v> I’d – yes, it did. I was 38 years old, my son was just two, I’d sold my business to Estée Lauder,

00:00:20.823 --> 00:00:24.144
I was having the time of my life, and a curve ball…it came from nowhere, I

00:00:24.169 --> 00:00:28.141
didn’t anticipate it, didn’t expect it, didn’t deserve it, but no one does.

00:00:28.523 --> 00:00:33.103
And I was, um, I’d found a lump in my breast, went to have it checked,

00:00:33.461 --> 00:00:37.168
thought it was a cyst and it wasn’t, it was a very, very aggressive form of breast cancer,

00:00:37.549 --> 00:00:41.471
and I was given a pretty grim diagnosis of under a year.

00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:45.345
And I remember sitting there and putting that same head on as [when]

00:00:45.371 --> 00:00:50.266
I was the little girl that stood on the chair and wouldn’t take no for an answer, and I thought,

00:00:50.291 --> 00:00:54.231
‘No one’s going to tell me when I’m going to die, I’m going to fight this and – and try and survive.’

00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:55.894
So, I went to New York City.

00:00:56.371 --> 00:00:59.730
I was one of the first women to take chemotherapy in a very different way,

00:00:59.764 --> 00:01:05.990
in much longer periods but shorter spaces of time, and I spent a year, surgery after surgery,

00:01:06.015 --> 00:01:11.700
lost my hair, lost half my body, and then found a way of putting my life back together again.

00:01:12.018 --> 00:01:16.754
Came through all of that, and was given…you’re not given the all-clear straightaway,

00:01:16.782 --> 00:01:19.970
but I was…I was told, you know, ‘It’s time to go back and live your life.’

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:24.859
And it’s like walking a tightrope without a net, it’s a really scary moment.

00:01:24.875 --> 00:01:28.972
And then I realized I couldn’t smell: that chemotherapy had taken away my sense of smell.

00:01:28.997 --> 00:01:30.664
<v><b>R:</b></v> How did that – how did you discover that?

00:01:31.031 --> 00:01:33.407
<v><b>J:</b></v> Because I couldn’t…All I could smell was this…I mean,

00:01:33.416 --> 00:01:37.933
I had been on Adriamycin, Cytoxan, Taxol, Taxotere – you name it, they’d thrown it.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.798
And let me tell you, I’m all clear still. It worked.

00:01:40.823 --> 00:01:41.183
<v><b>R:</b></v> Great, yeah.

00:01:41.208 --> 00:01:43.329
<v><b>J:</b></v> So, you have to balance life.

00:01:43.664 --> 00:01:48.338
But, all I could smell was metal, and all I could taste was metal, so I couldn’t create fragrance,

00:01:48.718 --> 00:01:52.613
and it was the most devastating thing for me, because that’s who I am.

00:01:52.638 --> 00:01:53.261
<v><b>R:</b></v> It’s your creativity.

00:01:53.277 --> 00:01:58.291
<v><b>J:</b></v> It’s – it’s how I interpret life, it’s…And so, I was left by this person,

00:01:58.316 --> 00:02:01.917
and that was why I decided to – to walk away and leave…um,

00:02:01.942 --> 00:02:04.584
I couldn’t bear to be around something I couldn’t be creative with,

00:02:04.609 --> 00:02:05.893
and I didn’t want to be the figurehead.

00:02:06.573 --> 00:02:10.325
And so, I made the decision to leave the business that I’d founded.

00:02:11.299 --> 00:02:13.371
<v><b>P:</b></v> Miraculously, five years later,

00:02:13.396 --> 00:02:17.208
Jo had regained her sense of smell and set up a new fragrance company.

00:02:17.796 --> 00:02:24.077
<v><b>J:</b></v> Well, 2011 I launched, er, Jo Loves, and, um, so, she’s seven years old now, and is now flying.

00:02:24.102 --> 00:02:27.092
But it – I promise you, it was a very tough journey getting there,

00:02:27.117 --> 00:02:33.362
and the first two years I wanted to quit every single day. It was just excruciatingly difficult.

00:02:33.387 --> 00:02:34.109
<v><b>R:</b></v> Harder the second time around…?

00:02:34.134 --> 00:02:34.705
<v><b>J:</b></v> Much harder.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:35.260
<v><b>R:</b></v> Why?

00:02:35.566 --> 00:02:38.981
<v><b>J:</b></v> Um, I made every mistake in the book, the second time round.

00:02:39.006 --> 00:02:41.585
I got the packaging wrong, I got the distribution wrong…I know, I know.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:42.110
<v><b>P:</b></v> But, why?

00:02:42.144 --> 00:02:45.312
<v><b>J:</b></v> I don’t know, I don’t know. We’re people, we’re human beings, we make mistakes. And when…

00:02:45.337 --> 00:02:48.206
<v><b>G:</b></v> Yeah…You don’t expect that of someone who’s been so successful, though.

00:02:48.237 --> 00:02:51.810
<v><b>J:</b></v> Well, that’s what the world thinks, but actually, in reality…

00:02:51.946 --> 00:02:55.056
But if I hadn’t done it, I hadn’t made those mistakes

00:02:55.558 --> 00:02:59.101
– I am living the most amazing adventure of life right at this moment.

00:02:59.404 --> 00:03:02.050
I wouldn’t be here. So, you learn from those mistakes, don’t you?

00:03:02.075 --> 00:03:03.326
<v><b>G:</b></v> You learn from your mistakes, exactly…